<h2>The contract and its collapse</h2>
<p>The agreement was operational infrastructure work of the most ordinary kind: maintaining optical fibre across six hill locations under a service-level regime. Its collapse followed the familiar script of public-contract disputes. The contractor's case is that the employer withheld the stores needed to perform, then terminated and blamed the contractor; the employer's case is that the contractor never mobilised at all. Each narrative, if true, makes the other side the author of the breach — and neither can be resolved on affidavits in a Section 11 proceeding.</p>

<h2>The Section 11(6) enquiry is deliberately narrow</h2>
<p>Since the 2015 amendment inserted Section 11(6A), and through the Supreme Court's subsequent case law, the referral court's jurisdiction has been confined to the existence of the arbitration agreement, leaving arbitrability and merits to the tribunal under the kompetenz-kompetenz principle in Section 16. Here both sides accepted that Clause 23 of the contract was an arbitration agreement, which made the application straightforward: the court recorded the rival positions on termination without engaging them, held the application deserved to be allowed, and made the appointment.</p>

<h2>Panel clauses and independent appointments</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>What the clause contemplated</h3>
    <p>For claims between Rs 5 lakh and Rs 5 crore, a sole arbitrator appointed by BSNL from its own panel, with the contractor choosing from a list of three names forwarded by BSNL — the employer curating the field.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>What the court did</h3>
    <p>Appointed an independent advocate as sole arbitrator directly, with the Section 11(8) disclosure to follow — placing neutrality of appointment beyond argument and insulating the eventual award from challenge on that ground.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>The order itself is economical and does not theorise; but its practical effect aligns with the direction of travel in Indian arbitration law, which has grown steadily more sceptical of appointment mechanisms controlled by one party — particularly government companies and PSUs — and more willing to secure appointments that satisfy the independence standards of Section 12 read with the Schedules.</p>

<h2>What the parties take into the arbitration</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>The store-supply question:</strong> whether the employer's obligation to provide materials was a condition of the contractor's performance, and who defaulted first.</li>
  <li><strong>The termination's validity:</strong> notice, cure opportunity and the contractual termination machinery will be measured against the SLA's terms.</li>
  <li><strong>Quantum:</strong> the order does not record the claim amount; heads such as work done, retention, security deposits and loss of profit remain open before the tribunal.</li>
  <li><strong>Timelines:</strong> the Section 29A clock will run from completion of pleadings, with the court signalling that the award should be endeavoured within the statutory period.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Contractors invoking arbitration against PSUs should serve the notice strictly in the clause's format — here, 60 days, with disputed points and amounts specified to the designated officer — before moving under Section 11(6), since compliance with the pre-reference procedure is the respondent's first objection. And in every court appointment, diarise the arbitrator's Section 11(8)/Section 12 disclosure: it is the foundation of the tribunal's legitimacy and of the award's resilience.</p>
</div>
